<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Massawa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Massawa is a port city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> coast of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>. It was a key outlet for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>'s trade, through which gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, slaves and aromatic resins were exported. Control of Massawa passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, which used it as a base for its colonial expansion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/massawa.jpg" alt="Massawa" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Reinhard Dietrich · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Massawa_harbour.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Massawa lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> at the northern end of the Gulf of Zula, beside the Dahlak Archipelago. The historical city stands on the islands of Basé, with the old centre, and Taulud, joined to each other and to the mainland by causeways. It seems to have emerged as a port between the 8th and 10th centuries, after the decline of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adulis">Adulis</a>, about 50 kilometres to the south.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>With the rise of the Sultanate of Dahlak in the 11th century, Massawa became the main link between the Muslim-dominated coast and the Christian highlands. From the 14th century highland rulers took more interest in it. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zara-yaqob">Zara Yaqob</a> appears to have reached it in the mid-15th century, and both Massawa and Dahlak were pillaged by the Abyssinians in 1464/65. The Portuguese gained a foothold at Massawa and Arkiko in 1513 as a port from which to reach allied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> captured Massawa in 1557 and tried to make it the capital of Habesh Eyalet, though they did not conquer the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>. In May 1865 the port passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khedive-of-egypt">Khedive of Egypt</a> with Ottoman consent, and the governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/werner-munzinger">Werner Munzinger</a> rebuilt it, with a customs house, a school, a hospital and causeways.</p><p>The Italians took the port in February 1885, with British agreement, and it was their colonial capital until Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinando-martini">Ferdinando Martini</a> moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asmara">Asmara</a> in 1897. They built a railway to Asmara and an Asmara-Massawa cableway, 75 kilometres long, which was the longest ropeway conveyor in the world at the time. A great earthquake in 1921 destroyed most of the city, and later buildings were of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reinforced-concrete">reinforced concrete</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Massawa was besieged unsuccessfully by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrean-liberation-front">Eritrean Liberation Front</a> in 1977–78 and was captured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrean-peoples-liberation-front">Eritrean People's Liberation Front</a> in February 1990, in an attack by land and sea known as Operation Fenkil. Most of the city was destroyed in the shelling and bombing that followed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>'s independence in 1991 left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> landlocked.</p><p>The old town keeps coral-stone houses, the Sheikh Hanafi Mosque of the 15th century, and the Imperial Palace built in 1872 to 1874 for Munzinger. A naval base, dhow docks, a railway station and an airport serve the city, and ferries run to the Dahlak Islands.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Massawa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Gold, ivory, enslaved people, civet, and aromatic resins went out through the port of <b>Massawa</b> in exchange for textiles and luxury goods, connecting Ethiopia to Egyptian, Arabian, and Indian Ocean commercial networks.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Together with <b>Massawa</b>, occupied in 1885, this grew into the colony of Eritrea, formally proclaimed in 1890, and was followed by the gradual establishment of Italian Somaliland on the Horn of Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/italian-empire">Italian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Italy united its possessions on the Red Sea coast, centred on <b>Massawa</b>, into the colony of Eritrea.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-1">1 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After Portuguese fleets entered the Red Sea, the Ottomans sent Ozdemir Pasha, who in 1555 created the province of Habesh (Abyssinia) and in 1557 seized <b>Massawa</b> and the Dahlak islands from the Sultanate of Dahlak.</blockquote><a href="/state/habesh-eyalet">Habesh Eyalet</a></li><li><blockquote>Italy occupied <b>Massawa</b> in 1885 with British approval and pushed inland, despite defeat at Dogali in 1887.</blockquote><a href="/state/italian-eritrea">Italian Eritrea</a></li><li><blockquote>Rebels captured <b>Massawa</b> in 1990 and advanced on the capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/peoples-democratic-republic-of-ethiopia">People's Democratic Republic of Ethiopia</a></li></ul></section>
